From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Il tuo nome <tu@esempio.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 13:17:46 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mb/lenovo/haswell: The Ethernet help text, with the restarts
 of a change

The help said that turning Ethernet back on needs a full power-down: it
was the measurement of a build that did not wake the PHY, and the wake
of LANPHYPC brings the controller back by itself. Say what a change
costs instead: up to three restarts at the next boot (one to clear the
lockdown, one to apply the bit, one if the MAC is still absent), done by
the firmware.

TEST=tools/cfr-dump.sh of ouroBIOS on the W541 build: the record
within the limit.

Change-Id: I6d9079deb8ac22f799b6d04a6031e32384f03424
---
 src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c | 7 ++-----
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c b/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c
index 4756ebe2ea..607218757e 100644
--- a/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c
+++ b/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c
@@ -746,11 +746,8 @@ static const struct sm_object onboard_ethernet = SM_DECLARE_BOOL({
 	.ui_helptext	= "The Gigabit Ethernet controller (Intel I217-LM).\n"
 			  "Default: on. Off: the controller is turned off in the\n"
 			  "chipset, for every OS and for network boot. A change takes\n"
-			  "effect with one extra restart, done by the firmware at the\n"
-			  "next boot (the memory may be trained again then). The\n"
-			  "setting is kept in the chipset: a firmware version without\n"
-			  "this option leaves the Ethernet as it was until it is\n"
-			  "turned on here again or the CMOS battery is removed.",
+			  "up to three restarts, which the firmware does by itself at\n"
+			  "the next boot.",
 	.default_value	= true,
 });
 
